															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/shaggydredlocks" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1722371); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1722371&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607460152" alt="Shaggydredlocks" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1722371)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/shaggydredlocks" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1722371); return false;" >Shaggydredlocks</a></span> <span class="odate time_1512098721 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Dec 2017 03:25</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3643401">
									

<p>This has all the hallmarks of something that's suitably creepy and massively appealing, but it feels like it's reaching in places; it seems like it's trying way too hard, which isn't very impressive.</p>
<p>nitpicks</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The original tape on which SCP-3139-1 is stored can be found in Containment Locker 017 on the uppermost floor of Site-882.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is oddly clunky when 'the original tape&#8230;' already has an SCP designation.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>unnamed &quot;host&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not sure why this needed quotemarks here or anywhere else. Ditto for &quot;studio&quot; later on.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Multiple individuals are heard laughing throughout the tape, suggesting it was either filmed in front of or presented to a live audience.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yet, it goes on to repeatedly reference the audience - it'd probably be less obtuse if the description were better structured.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>The structure depicted in the footage has yet to be located by the Foundation and is speculated to be a construct of the anomaly itself.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Based off what, exactly? It's just thrown out there with no foundation. This is where it begins to tip from 'show' into 'tell'.</p>
<hr />
<p>Quick aside, 3139&#8212;1-A is easily the most interesting aspect of this article.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Viewer's biological child (before and after their birth)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Aside from being an odd way to phrase this ( &quot;Viewer's biological child (including fetuses)&quot; may flow better.), this seems like it's trying too hard (the fetal victims). If this were something subtle weaved in for fridge horror, it might have been more satisfying.</p>
<p>Plus, their inclusion throws several spanners in how this works: How would the host/audience refer to someone who has yet to be named? How would fetuses or infants, even being scaled up to adults, possess the mental capacity or physical dexterity to attempt escape from a maze, or even operate their new bodies?</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Should SCP-3139-1-B escape the structure (and subsequently the show), no further anomalous effects manifest.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because it's not commented upon, am I to assume people are de-aged? Or do full-grown adults suddenly explode out of pregnant women?</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Should SCP-3139-1-B be captured by SCP-3139-1-A, they will cease to exist in reality.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>they will cease to exist in reality. Save for the viewer,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Conveniently, Foundation personnel are unaffected by this, because then how else can they provide exposition. More magic knowledge that's making the concept seem overwritten.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Some viewers have reported SCP-3139-1-B attempting to communicate with them, though this is inaudible over the music and sound effects of SCP-3139-1.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Neat detail that's stale after so much dry clinical prose. Had this [article as a whole] been condensed into something punchier, or if this were presented in another way, or if there was something earlier to build atmosphere, this may have been more striking, but I was already getting bored by this point.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>possessing general physical similarities, suggesting they are unborn children</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure what this is supposed to mean.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>In these instances, the viewer's attempts to bear children (if female) or fertilize female eggs (if male) will always fail despite showing no signs of clinical sterility. No known methods to counteract this are currently known.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why? This just seems random.</p>
<hr />
<p>Video starts off fine, but I didn't really get anything out of it. Seemed over-and-done without doing much in the way to build suspense or add atmosphere. The final line is what really elicited a groan:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We claim another. We claim another. Glorious prey. Your [inaudible] are delicious. Delicious.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Which seemed narmy, generic, and extremely unsatisfying.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>TLDR: There are far too many issues with this for me to get invested. Between contrived exposition, needless complexity, poorly-structured information, and try-hard spoopiness, I've got no choice but to downvote.</p>

							</div>

								
					
